    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d147">

                <pb facs="0190" n="180" sameAs="frus:frus1945v03/pg180"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">740.00119 Control (Germany)/1–3145</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Assistant Secretary of State</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Dunn</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Assistant
                        Secretary of War</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">McCloy</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Washington</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-02-03">February 3,
                        1945</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Dear Jack</hi>: In reply to your letter of January
                    31, 1945, enclosing a suggested cable to Winant with respect to the zones of
                    occupation in Germany, I have not taken any action on this in view of the
                    developments <pb facs="0191" n="181" xml:id="pg181"/> on February 1, which were
                    set forth in a cable of that date from the Secretary of State, a paraphrase of
                    which I have sent to you.<anchor n="52" xml:id="d147-f1"/>
                </p>
                <p>Sincerely yours,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">James Clement Dunn</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d147-f1" n="52" xml:id="d147-f1-content">
                <p>Cricket 45, February 1, from the Secretary of State at Malta, reporting the U.S.
                    and U.K. approval of the zones of occupation protocol, p. 179. On February 6,
                    1945, at Yalta, the Combined Chiefs of Staff approved the agreement regarding
                    the Bremen-Bremerhaven enclave. For text, see <ref target="frus1945Malta#pg_639">
                        <hi rendition="#italic">Conferences at Malta and Yalta</hi>, 1945, p.
                        639</ref>
                </p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
